WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.300
- Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.080
Welcome to the Chubb Fellowship lecture

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.940
with Shah Rukh Khan in collaboration

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.390
with the South Asian Society.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.980
In 1949, Yale alumnus, Hendon Chubb

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.710
founded a speaking fellowship

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.400
to promote the notion that service

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.740
to the public good is the highest calling

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.940
to which the citizen can aspire.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.160
According to visiting speaker, to be named

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.180
a Chubb Fellow is Yale's
most notable honor.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.164
Chubb Fellows have included
national heads of state,

00:00:35.164 --> 00:00:38.940
international leaders, Nobel
and Pulitzer Prize winners,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.090
and a wide range of highly
accomplished individuals

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.450
in politics, business,
science, and the arts.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.510
The experiences and leadership
of these individuals

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.170
have helped defined the
challenges of our times,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.753
and the way that we should
seek to address them.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.150
All of us at Yale are exceptionally proud

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.584
to be the first American
university to honor Mr. Khan.

00:01:02.584 --> 00:01:05.584
(audience cheering)

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.740
Mr. Khan, we awaited
for your arrival eagerly

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.090
and having you with us is
nothing short of a dream.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.306
I'm sure everyone in the
audience agrees with me too.

00:01:18.306 --> 00:01:20.940
(audience cheering)

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.740
Let me just get a general sense.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.140
How many have you all have dreamt

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.203
of seeing Mr. Shah Rukh Khan?

00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:30.010
(audience cheering)

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:34.213
Ladies and gentlemen,
all lucky 1,700 of you,

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.793
imagine 300,000 times a
number of people in this room.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.370
Around three billion people, three times

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.950
the population of India, are approximately

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.210
one in every two people in the world.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.090
That is the number of people

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.983
that recognize Mr. Khan globally.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.390
11,
(audience applauding)

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.730
11 of his movies have grossed
over a billion dollars.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.600
He's been nominated for some 200 awards,

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.100
won around 150, of which 11 have been

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.010
the prestigious Best Actor Filmfare Award.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.390
And of course, in addition to all of this,

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:14.332
he's stolen the heart of
countless and countless girls.

00:02:14.332 --> 00:02:17.660
(audience cheering)

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.310
It is only natural that he's fondly

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.100
referred to as King Khan, not only

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.910
has he ruled Bollywood
for the last two decades,

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.610
but he's also reign over the hearts

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.363
of millions and millions of people.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.640
I want to share a little story

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.710
about how wonderful a person Mr. Khan is.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.680
A very long time ago, when I
was a newly admitted Yalie,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.150
I excitedly asked Mr. Khan
if he would come visit Yale.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.490
And he'd very politely
said yes and expressed

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.330
how important it was to him
to engage with students.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.170
This year, when I was elected president

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.610
of the South Asian Society, I decided

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.610
to take him up on his offer and so

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.944
over winter break I asked him again,

00:02:56.944 --> 00:03:00.107
and he very graciously
said, "Anytime you call me."

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.470
And so I waited for April,
which is our South Asian

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.520
event this month, to
invite him, not realizing

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.003
that April is the busiest
month on his schedule.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.240
Yet, because of the commitment he'd made

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.000
four years ago, Mr. Khan took time off

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.710
to fly all the way to
New Haven from Bombay

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:19.937
just to be with us for the day.

00:03:19.937 --> 00:03:23.690
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.440
It is this amazing quality
of going out of his way

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.090
to spread happiness that will never

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.410
let Mr. Khan leave our dreams.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.630
Speaking of dreams, how
many of y'all have dreamt

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.280
of seeing Mr. Khan but never thought

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.452
it'll translate into reality?

00:03:36.452 --> 00:03:39.010
(audience cheering)

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.860
There are a few people
who need to be thanked

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.330
for translating this dream into reality.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:45.280
First and most foremost, Master Brenzel

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.050
and Timothy Dwight College,
(audience applauding)

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.510
without whom, this event would have

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.600
never been at the scale that it is.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.020
Mr. George Joseph, the
Office of International

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:55.920
Affairs and the India Initiative,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.780
for the immense patience
and generous support.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.470
The South Asia Film Society for running

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.170
the Shah Rukh Khan Film Festival.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.480
The Yale Office of Public Affairs

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.900
for helping realize this mammoth event.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.860
My South Asian Society bod

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:14.550
for all the hard work and
taking SAS to new levels.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.750
Our membership for being so
enthusiastic about our events,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.900
and last but not the least,
I'd like to thank my mother.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.583
And Shah Rukh Khan for
making this dream come true.

00:04:24.505 --> 00:04:25.510
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:29.433
Ladies and gentlemen,
(audience cheering)

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.940
it is our honor to present to you

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.430
for the first time in
an American university,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.933
the Badshah of Bollywood,
King Khan, Shah Rukh Khan.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.540
(audience cheering)

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.560
(audience cheering)

00:05:25.327 --> 00:05:27.160
- Thank you very much.

00:05:31.014 --> 00:05:33.565
Thank you, thank you,
thank you very much and.

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:36.565
(audience cheering)

00:05:38.815 --> 00:05:39.648
Thank you, thank you very much,

00:05:39.648 --> 00:05:41.620
it's really nice to be here and--

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.073
- I love you!
- I love you too.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.870
Good evening, it's really
nice to be here, finally.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:51.870
Yeah, it was a long
flight, thank you, Nita,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:52.800
for getting me here, thank you,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.300
Isha, for having me over here.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.920
We were detained at the airport as always

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.350
for an hour an a half, which was nice.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.820
- Boo!
- Yeah, it always happened.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:01.840
It's nice, you know, whenever I start

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.330
feeling too arrogant about myself,

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.385
I always take a trip to America.

00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:07.189
(audience laughing)

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:12.160
(laughs) Yeah, the immigration guys

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.610
kick the star out of stardom.

00:06:14.536 --> 00:06:15.399
(audience laughing)

00:06:15.399 --> 00:06:16.990
But, I have my small victories.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.980
They always ask me how tall I am,

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:22.075
and I always lie and get
away, five feet 10 inches.

00:06:22.075 --> 00:06:22.908
(audience laughing)

00:06:22.908 --> 00:06:24.100
Next time I'm gonna be more adventurous.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.100
What color are you, I'm gonna say White.

00:06:27.074 --> 00:06:28.225
(audience laughing)

00:06:28.225 --> 00:06:29.920
But okay, I'm supposed
to be serious, yeah,

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.201
because this is for students and kids,

00:06:32.201 --> 00:06:33.990
so first of all let me
just thank the faculty

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.336
and students of the Yale University,

00:06:36.336 --> 00:06:39.020
fellows of Timothy Dwight
College and the Chubb Fellowship,

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.080
and Master Jeffrey Brenzel
for being so kind, thank you.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.440
Yale Office of International Affairs

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.530
and the Yale's South Asian Society,

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.810
and of course Isha for
dealing and following up

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:51.870
with the most disorganized
and incommunicative

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.170
person in the world, which is me,

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:54.420
to fix today's meeting with you all.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.280
I'm really, really happy, very humble,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.249
very honored, and thank you
for having me over here.

00:06:59.249 --> 00:07:03.011
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:07:03.011 --> 00:07:04.450
Look, I have memories of,
I've been here before.

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:06.280
Five years ago, I was here in Yale.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.380
I was doing the song, some of the shots,

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:11.333
the ones at the graveyard,
they were shot here.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.703
No, I'm being honest here.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.080
And it was very cold, it was I think fifth

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.020
or sixth of December, it was snowing here,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.190
and I remember I was doing a song

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.981
from Kabhi Alvida Naa Kehna in the middle

00:07:22.981 --> 00:07:25.610
(audience cheering)

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:27.770
and I was trying to kiss Rani Mukerji

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.440
while mouthing the words,
we do that in Bollywood

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.807
for all the guys who don't know how it is.

00:07:33.807 --> 00:07:35.240
Yeah, we sing songs and
we try to kiss girls

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.968
at the same time.
(audience laughing)

00:07:37.968 --> 00:07:39.740
And I'm being honest, my mouth froze

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.137
in the middle of (sings in Hindi).

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.200
I had a locked jaw.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.380
I'm hoping my second outing
to Yale is a little better,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.120
because otherwise it
just won't sound right

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:52.160
if I was to get stuck on.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.133
I don't know what you guys call yourself,

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:55.831
Yaletides, Yalers?

00:07:55.831 --> 00:07:57.577
- [Audience] Yalie.

00:07:57.577 --> 00:08:00.690
- Uh, yeah, that, my, I
hope I don't get stuck

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:03.240
at Yah and go beyond that, eh?

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.580
So I was told not to dwell
too much on my movies

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.260
when I spoke to you, I'm to give you

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.080
an inspirational talk, tell you stuff

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.330
you can think about when
you leave this room.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.040
That worries me, it gives
me performance anxiety,

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.410
about 1,500 of you here hoping to hear

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.831
words of wisdom from
the sexy, desirable man,

00:08:19.831 --> 00:08:23.390
(audience cheering)

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.590
who couldn't kiss a girl
last time he was in Yale

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.110
because it was too cold.
(audience laughing)

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.520
But I am, I wanna tell you
right in the beginning,

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:31.540
I'm not that guy.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.630
I'm telling you, I mean
I'm sexy and desirable,

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.080
but I'm,
(audience cheering)

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.250
but I'm not about to leave
you any more inspired

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.870
than you were when you walked in here.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.900
And I read this lame joke on Google,

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.350
yes I go to Google and
search everything there,

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.789
even my next script I'm
searching on Google now.

00:08:46.789 --> 00:08:48.113
(audience laughing)

00:08:48.113 --> 00:08:49.220
And the joke was like this, a dying man

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.850
gasping for breath, desperately gestured

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.000
to the priest by his side
for a piece of paper.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.720
With great effort, he then
wrote a few words on it,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.040
handed to the priest, and passed away.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.980
The priest kept the paper in his pocket

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.550
and forgot all about it
until the final service.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.730
Here, he suddenly recalled
the dead man's last scribble.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.900
Unfolding the paper, he told
the funeral congregation

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.320
that he was about to read great words

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.850
of inspiration from the dying man.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.410
He opened the paper, the piece of paper

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.530
had these words on it, you're standing

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.834
on my oxygen tube, you fool.

00:09:17.834 --> 00:09:20.220
(audience laughing)

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.740
So I'm gonna be kinda like that priest.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.290
Don't expect words of wisdom from me.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:27.810
I'm just gonna tell you my life's journey

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.680
in very simple words, and which
may not leave you inspired,

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.350
but will help you survive this life.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.750
And if you can do that
kids, if you can survive,

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:38.590
happiness, creativity and
success will follow on its own.

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.230
Only I hope my words will
give you enough insight

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.470
when you hear the story
that I have to tell you,

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.210
that you can tell the world, world,

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.290
move over, you're standing
on my oxygen tube,

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.360
I need to breathe, yeah.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.170
So journeys can be defined by age and time

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:56.010
or even by destinations
as most often they are,

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.360
but I feel it's hard for me to tell you

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.160
the story of my life in those terms

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.380
because the concept of
time has always eluded me.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.530
The day my father died seemed longer

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:07.180
than my entire childhood.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.700
The day I felt my first success seemed

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.580
fleetingly are long,
not long enough perhaps.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.180
I wonder where it went.

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:15.840
Even the cycle of time confounds me.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.630
I worked at night, I worked
the dark until sunrise

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.570
on most days and fall asleep
as the world awakens to light.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.580
My friends call me an owl.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.840
I like to think of myself as bat,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.363
Batman, the prince of darkness.

00:10:28.363 --> 00:10:30.010
(audience cheering)

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.730
Some of you would know, I
have a superhero fetish,

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.180
yeah, so I keep bringing this back.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.120
Age is not my forte either.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.240
I still cannot fix my own, am I 45 or 15?

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:41.980
If I could, would I be romancing girls,

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.580
one third my age would
normally would call me uncle.

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:48.380
I had so much fun collecting
the action figures

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.202
of my last film called Ra.One, that,

00:10:51.202 --> 00:10:53.250
(audience cheering)

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.590
that none of the critical reviews

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.123
tanking my film affected me at all.

00:10:57.123 --> 00:10:58.570
- Boo!
- Yeah boo!

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:01.310
- And as for my destination,
I don't think I ever knew one.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.810
I walk, I run in the
direction of my dreams,

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:05.630
things change along
the way, people change,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:08.860
I change, the world changes,
and even my dreams change.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:11.200
I don't have a place to
arrive, I just keep doing

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.650
what I know how to do the
best that I can do it.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.590
I'll probably ended up diluted, geriatric,

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:18.720
in a wheelchair wearing a cape and tights,

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:20.840
imaging my own flight out of this world,

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.334
but of course with a
young girl in my arms.

00:11:23.334 --> 00:11:24.931
(audience laughing)
So I'll tell you

00:11:24.931 --> 00:11:26.570
the story of me, but I'll
tell it in my own way.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:28.500
In the language of my perceptions.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.320
In the things I think matter beyond fame,

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.180
success, and the dyeing of my gray hair.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.110
I have understood that
the measure of my life

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:37.720
lies in the expanse of
my heart's experience,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.280
and nothing else matters.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.307
If you take anything out of it, good.

00:11:41.307 --> 00:11:44.000
Otherwise, I've been told by the master,

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.147
I can put on music and dance
for you on Chammak Challo.

00:11:48.069 --> 00:11:51.069
(audience cheering)

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.700
Okay, so first of all,
yeah, we do that later,

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.170
I know, I know, people call me to these

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.200
serious places, and say
oh, fuck everything,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.950
just do the dance and go back home.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.711
But I insist you listen to me, okay?

00:12:05.711 --> 00:12:08.210
(audience laughing)

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.520
I wanna tell you, I have to tell you

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:12.820
that I learned a few big words

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.785
because I was coming to Yale.

00:12:14.785 --> 00:12:17.230
(audience laughing)
On Google, of course.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.770
And I (laughs), so the first one

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.219
that I'm gonna tell you about,

00:12:21.219 --> 00:12:22.052
so there are three things I wanna

00:12:22.052 --> 00:12:23.710
tell you about this evening.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:24.970
The first one is I'm gonna tell you

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:27.230
to be a funambulist.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:29.530
Now, yeah, that's a big
word, yeah, impressed?

00:12:30.681 --> 00:12:32.650
Okay, so that means a tightrope walker.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.970
Because a lot of you
people, actually all of you,

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:38.560
are somehow the, are going to
create things in your life.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.690
So a funambulist is
like a tightrope walker.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.090
The first one I learned,
please give me claps

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.444
for this new word that I've taught Yale.

00:12:45.444 --> 00:12:48.611
(audience applauding)

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:52.110
Okay, however I look at it
in this eventual analysis,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.490
my life has centered around my creativity.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.710
I have assimilated the world
through creative expression,

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:59.390
and in return the world has
experienced me hopefully.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:01.040
I've grown to understand that on one hand,

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:02.610
the world will always uphold creativity

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:05.030
as the most honest feeling possible.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:06.900
On the other hand, the potence of fame,

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.420
the glitz, the glamor,
the wealth that arise

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.690
from this very recognition of creativity

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:13.990
will always be questioned.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.250
Why do we do that?

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:17.240
Because sometimes, it
allows us to feel better

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.100
than the creator, and sometimes,

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.390
it fills a void within
us, that comes about

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:24.120
by being in awe of his or her creation.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.500
Either way, it enables us to
quantify his or her engagement,

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.610
engagement with the world around him.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:30.650
I'm an actor.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.510
My life is a testament to this duality.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:37.400
George Burns said that
acting is all about honesty.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.298
If you can fake that, you've got it made.

00:13:40.298 --> 00:13:42.440
And he couldn't have defined it better.

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:44.590
Honest and fake, yes, that's what I feel

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:46.910
as a creative person all the time.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.110
Let me tell you my schizophrenia.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:50.740
Creative expression comes from the deepest

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.690
experience of the artist himself.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:55.110
A good artist cannot be
separated from his creation.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.050
Good art is honest art.

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.140
A man may be an artist,
a writer, a sculpture,

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.250
an actor, or a totem pole cover.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:04.800
Whatever he is, if what he
creates is true to himself,

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.840
it becomes a vivid testimonial
to human creativity.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:10.610
If it lacks honesty, it is entire,

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:12.870
its entire premise is a waste.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.860
At the same time, and quite paradoxically,

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.050
a man becomes distinct from his creation

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:20.210
from the moment it is
placed in the public domain.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.890
It no longer even belongs to him.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:24.930
So it comes from your gut,

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:26.140
and it is put out there for others

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:28.730
to accept it or throw it in the gutter.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:30.240
Many a nights, I have gone back home

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.620
after receiving an award,
pumped up and all happy,

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:35.577
and just to read on the Internet

00:14:35.577 --> 00:14:38.010
that what I really deserved
was the Golden Banana

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.714
for the Worst Actor of the Year Award.

00:14:40.714 --> 00:14:41.710
- Boo!
- Yeah, it's called

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.744
Kela award or some shit like that.

00:14:43.744 --> 00:14:45.646
(audience laughing)

00:14:45.646 --> 00:14:49.270
And I, I become heartbroken, angry,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.300
and completely convinced
that bananas and critics

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.150
both should have their skins peeled

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:58.007
and fed to the monkeys.
(audience cheering)

00:14:58.007 --> 00:15:02.340
I momentarily lose my
ability to give and close up.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.200
And here's where the trick is.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:06.650
When you are in this place of despair,

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.710
when you walk out of this
college, this university

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.100
and walk the path of life, where the world

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:13.300
is tearing you down into yourself,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.460
there's only one thing
you can do to survive.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.200
Hang on to who you are inside.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:19.890
The world will be unkind to you.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.540
It will not be able to see you.

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:25.090
You must learn at such times
to be able to see yourself.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:26.890
Life as a creative person is like

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:29.120
walking on a tightrope, I have to

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.100
keep balancing, I have
to keep the balance,

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:34.300
I begin to lose myself
in my own melodrama.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.010
It is frustrating that I find myself

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.730
living up to other people's interpretation

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:39.530
of what I ought to be.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:42.010
And when faced with
dissent or un-appreciation,

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.710
I start losing my love
affair with my own audience.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.970
It becomes a tight balance act
to keep doing what I do best

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:49.530
and not be bothered by the reactions

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.050
of the people I do it
for in the first place.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.600
I danced harder, I (mumbles) longer,

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.020
and I pivoted on my road, stretched dot

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:00.040
beneath my feet, and I try not to slip.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:01.980
I can slide but never fall.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.260
And all this while, I have
to keep a smile on my face

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.660
and keep signing autographs
and taking pictures.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:08.800
And why do I do this?

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.890
Because I'm a funambulist,
trying to balance

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:14.070
my action, and exceed reaction

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:16.763
to my naked show of who I am inside.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:19.730
Yet when I'm playing this
real life illusion out,

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:22.460
more often than not, my
honest self is sitting

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.750
in the audience applauding my performance,

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.413
while laughing heartily
at my own stupidity.

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:31.120
My little kids and friends here

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:33.120
learn to love at yourself too.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.990
Never become cynical about
yourself or your life.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:37.210
Becoming cynical about your life

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.490
is the single most
destructive thing you can do.

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:43.780
For you have to remember,
creativity is your gift

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:46.040
to the world, it was
never meant to be butter

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:48.649
for anything, not even appreciation.

00:16:48.649 --> 00:16:50.240
You have to dig deep.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.380
I do it, I don't know if
I'm supposed to say this,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.000
I do it while drinking vodka and listening

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.640
to really soddy songs.
(audience laughing)

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:00.700
But you guys have to find less
destructive ways to do this.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:02.300
At least till you become my age.

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:05.550
But you have to believe that you create

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.510
only because this is the biggest gift

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.180
you have to give to your world.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:13.140
And maybe that's why we
even call God a creator.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:15.580
It's not about the cars
or houses, it never was.

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:16.910
These are peripherals.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:18.660
They never come about
because of your talent

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.360
or your creative
outpourings, they come out

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:22.850
of a business that people around you do.

00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:25.850
Those people are in the
business of butter, not you.

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.880
Yours is the business
of giving and learning.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:30.640
Your work of art may never
be complete in your lifetime.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:31.920
Your fulfillment will always lie

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.690
in your creative expression,
not in its product.

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.870
So look beyond the
brick beds, the critics,

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:38.990
and know within you, that
you always have a choice

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:40.930
between butter and creation.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:43.610
Life as a creator will
always be a tightrope.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:46.720
So do not try to feed your
stomach with creativity.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:50.070
It is food for the soul, not your stomach.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.850
Do not be afraid to defy conventions.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:54.620
Do not be afraid to destroy systems

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:56.980
that kill art and your souls.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.280
Do not be afraid to be hungry.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.150
And do not be afraid to
walk alone if necessary.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.700
Because on a tightrope, we all walk alone.

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:09.210
And remember, if you're a
creator, you're a funambulist.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.740
And not very many people
know what it means,

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:14.820
let alone be it, so be a funambulist

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.660
as my prayer to all you
creative people here

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:20.680
at Yale tonight.
(audience applauding)

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:26.980
The second thing I wanna tell you,

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:28.980
and I'll go over it fast before you get

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.320
too bored, is love your punching bags.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.910
Just as my life is
centered around creativity,

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:35.710
like every fellow human beings, it is also

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:38.610
centered around the
wish to find happiness.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.010
Your age is the age when we most

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:41.760
confuse happiness with gratification,

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.620
so I will quite plainly tell you.

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.921
If you're smart, if you
want to survive life,

00:18:45.921 --> 00:18:48.880
relentless onslaught of challenges,

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:50.470
you will sooner or later understand

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.800
that the things that make
you happy 10 years ago

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:54.500
will end up being the
ones that make you happy

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.420
when you hit the geriatrics
of age stage also.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.353
It's a good start collecting
your action figure heroes now.

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:04.290
I have everything I could
have aspired for at your age.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.490
I have success, I have
fame, I have wealth,

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:10.820
and I have three PlayStations.
(audience laughing)

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.859
I do, I have one for the
house, one for shootings,

00:19:13.859 --> 00:19:16.846
and one just in case,
because I can have one more.

00:19:16.846 --> 00:19:20.608
(audience laughing)

00:19:20.608 --> 00:19:21.863
But none of these,

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:28.950
but none of these have any
consequence to my happiness.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.663
The only thing that does it
is the love of my children.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:35.034
You don't have children.

00:19:35.034 --> 00:19:37.113
(audience laughing)
I hope.

00:19:40.884 --> 00:19:45.793
(laughs) Not yet, and if you
have, don't tell the master.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.450
But you have parents, and
you have people you love.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:52.030
And nothing in this world of every things

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.240
means more than that, happiness,

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:55.680
in other word, lies in the things

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.860
you will never be able to count.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:59.650
To me it is no more
than cuddling to my kids

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:02.453
and watching iCarly, or the Family Guy.

00:20:03.317 --> 00:20:04.938
(audience cheering)

00:20:04.938 --> 00:20:07.630
I think Family Guy's very cool.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:09.563
And I think iCarly's also very sweet.

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:12.470
Most of the time I do that.

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:14.000
The other day, my son and I stumbled upon

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.153
Kama Sutra on the net.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:20.430
And I can tell you, that
experience was not very happy.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:25.063
He's 14 and he knew more
about the poses than I did.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.250
That's not fair.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:32.460
I want you to understand this business

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.490
of happiness well because
I know at one level,

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:36.360
all parents are actually the same.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.650
Some look stunner, some are less fun,

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:42.030
some embarrassingly weird,
but for each parent,

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:44.630
the bottom and the top line
of their lives is this,

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:47.870
you kids are the greatest
source of happiness.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.710
Parents want nothing in return,

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:51.960
just that you respect
that feeling, that's all.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:55.040
I'd like to narrate an
incident of my own children.

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:56.360
First of all, let me be very clear,

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.493
I do believe girls are
from the planet Venus.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.580
My girl comes from a place
of gentleness, caring,

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.550
love, intelligent, and
all things beautiful.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.493
My boy comes from, I'm too
good to be your son planet.

00:21:10.832 --> 00:21:12.630
(audience laughing)
And I know, I know,

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:13.990
this may make me very unpopular

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:16.720
but I wanted to say this
while I was flying here,

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.050
that if one thing that you
take away from this lecture

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:22.673
tonight, I want everyone to
know, boys are obnoxious.

00:21:22.673 --> 00:21:25.673
(audience cheering)

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:32.260
They are philandering, lying, cheating,

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:35.753
super zealous, sneering, snobs.

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:38.033
(audience cheering)

00:21:38.033 --> 00:21:39.093
Girls are good.

00:21:44.863 --> 00:21:47.446
(speaks Hindi)

00:21:51.123 --> 00:21:52.410
But I believe that, I like girls.

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:54.285
I think girls are much better.

00:21:54.285 --> 00:21:55.210
I was in London shooting
and missing my kids,

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:56.570
and I'll give you an incident.

00:21:56.570 --> 00:21:58.220
Being from the boring school of people

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.500
who send writings to their kids

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:01.950
in the hope of making
them better human beings,

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:03.770
I send my daughter this verse from a poem

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:07.470
by E. E. Cummings, and you
guys should read the poem,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:08.303
it's fantastic.

00:22:08.303 --> 00:22:10.340
And this poem is for all the girls here.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:11.173
I want to read this out.

00:22:11.173 --> 00:22:15.690
I do not know what it is about
you that closes and opens,

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:18.270
only something in me understands.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.610
The voice of your eyes
is deeper than all roses.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:25.430
Nobody, not even the rain
has such small hands.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:27.730
And this for all you girls,
and the only smaller hands

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.156
is a guess this little
kid who's crying there.

00:22:30.156 --> 00:22:31.934
(audience laughing)

00:22:31.934 --> 00:22:34.270
(laughs) I send this
message to my daughter

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:37.000
and I instantly receive
this text message in return.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.610
I love it, Papa, it is beautiful.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:40.500
I'm going to write it in my secret diary

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:43.590
with the secret lock and keep
it in my secret hiding place

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.693
under the Katy Perry and Lady Gaga poster.

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:50.670
I love you and I miss you.

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:53.906
I'm too excited watching
Hunger Games tonight.

00:22:53.906 --> 00:22:56.906
(audience cheering)

00:22:58.198 --> 00:23:01.800
(audience laughing)

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:05.890
And (laughs) feeling bad, feeling bad

00:23:07.337 --> 00:23:08.520
that I hadn't texted something meaningful

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.343
to my darling son, I sent
him something I had read too.

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.760
How are you my son, I wrote, I missed you.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:15.960
Do you know, a boy is someone

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:17.610
that a mother loves the most?

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.410
Little girls hate him.

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:21.470
He is true to a dirt on its face,

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:23.460
beauty with the cut on his finger.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:25.280
Wisdom would smell in his hair,

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:28.123
and hope of the future
with a frog in his pocket.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:30.730
I love you.

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:35.227
He replied back with one
letter of the alphabet.

00:23:35.227 --> 00:23:36.910
- [Audience] K!

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:38.600
- Nah, it's actually
it's very close to you.

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:41.320
It had a but in front, and says but,

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:44.978
and he had the Y of Yale, why.

00:23:44.978 --> 00:23:47.228
(audience laughing)

00:23:47.228 --> 00:23:49.530
I mean like,

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.073
why are you sending such boring messages?

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:55.728
And of course there was an emoticon,

00:23:55.728 --> 00:23:57.400
one of those squiggly little things

00:23:57.400 --> 00:24:00.540
which I can't make out
what they're expressing.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:03.720
I wanted to fly to Mumbai
and hang him up side down

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:06.303
till he look like a silly
faced emoticon himself.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:11.040
But I didn't, I just
smiled, and both replies

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.000
made me feel love for my kids.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.660
Whatever they do as long as they're happy,

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:16.070
it makes me happy.

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:19.500
I speak to you guys, a parent
of two very weird kids,

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.020
whatever you do, whichever mistake

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:22.580
you make, however you react to them,

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:24.800
your parents are your best friends.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:26.910
They might be boring,
silly, or stern at times,

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:28.750
maybe some of you are
embarrassed of yours,

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:31.150
I know my kids are of me, but if ever,

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.310
any of you are in trouble, of any kind,

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:34.700
the best friends you can always trust

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:36.590
to watch your backs are your parents.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:38.350
They will always come good.

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:40.160
I lost my parents very early in my life,

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:41.930
and I miss them dearly, so all of you

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:44.151
who still have yours,
don't listen to them,

00:24:44.151 --> 00:24:48.603
fool them if you must, a bit
of lying is also welcomed,

00:24:48.603 --> 00:24:52.400
(audience laughing)
but make sure you cherish

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.970
what you have because when
you don't have them like me,

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:57.380
you really miss someone to be rude to,

00:24:57.380 --> 00:25:00.450
someone to you can, someone
you can take for granted.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.950
Someone to send whatever you wish with.

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:05.883
You miss the comfort of
being loved unconditionally.

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:10.060
I call parents unconditional
and forgiving punching bags

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:15.860
who feel happiest when they
get bashed up by the kids.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:17.990
If you want to survive
life, it's best to begin

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.200
to respect the gift of love right now.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:21.975
As children, your first teachers

00:25:21.975 --> 00:25:24.280
of this acceptance are your parents.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:26.000
If you're unable to accept
the love they give you

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.220
in whatever form it arise,
even if it's in the form

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:29.993
of a tight slap across your faces,

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:33.570
then when you become a
parent, you will end up

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:36.060
having to learn this lesson
somewhat more harshly

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:38.240
from teachers you give birth to.

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:39.600
Those are your kids.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:41.980
And learning Kama Sutra from
my son is not a great idea,

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:45.300
you would agree.
(audience laughing)

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:46.160
Incidentally, he studies in the school

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:47.733
that Isha's mom runs in India.

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:51.760
I have to say, Ma'm, your
syllabus is quite different

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:53.910
from the one I used to
study when I was 14.

00:25:55.284 --> 00:25:58.810
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:00.710
She's insisting that she will get

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:02.563
Kama Sutra in Yale also very soon.

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:09.074
And the last bit, a fiendish
friend called failure.

00:26:09.074 --> 00:26:11.990
Whether I like it or not,
my life has also been

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:15.630
in constant play with what
the world calls success.

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:18.220
Success is a wonderful thing,
but it tends not be the sort

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:19.883
of experience that we learn from.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:22.910
We enjoy it, perhaps we even deserve it,

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.320
but we don't acquire wisdom from it.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:27.870
And maybe that's why it
cannot be passed on either.

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:29.320
Me being successful does not mean

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:30.750
my kids are going to be successful.

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:31.850
Even if I teach them everything

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:33.720
that I know and how to do it.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.280
So I feel that talking about success

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.570
is completely a big waste of time.

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:39.100
Instead, let me tell you very honestly,

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:40.360
whatever happened to me happened

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:43.740
because I've always been
terrified of failure.

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.140
I don't want as much to succeed

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:47.530
as much as I don't want to fail.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:49.530
I come from a very normal lower middle

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:52.490
class family, I saw a lot of failure.

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:54.200
My father was a beautiful man

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.450
and the most successful
failure in the world.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:00.880
My mother also failed to
stay long enough with me

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.180
to see me become a big movie star.

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:05.140
We were quite poor actually,
and let me tell you,

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:08.660
poverty is not ennobling
experience at all.

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:10.460
Poverty, and tears, fear, and stress,

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:12.210
and sometimes depression.

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.170
I watched my parents go
through this several times.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:16.980
At an early age after my parents died,

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:19.600
I equated poverty with failure.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:21.100
I just didn't want to be poor.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:24.060
So when I got a chance to act in films,

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:26.140
it wasn't out of any creative desire,

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.710
I say this honestly.

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:30.950
It was poorly out of the
fear of failure and poverty.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:32.670
Most of the films I signed were discards

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:34.650
of better known actors, and the producers

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:37.140
could not find anyone else to do them.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:38.510
I did them all to make sure that I

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:41.380
was working to avoid unemployment.

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.756
The timing was something was right,

00:27:43.756 --> 00:27:45.000
I worked very hard, there
were other people around it,

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:48.740
and I became a big star,
the films became big hits.

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:51.570
Which means sometimes, a success is not

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:55.010
the direct result of actions,
and let nobody tell you that.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:58.810
Success sometimes just happens, really.

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:01.930
It is accidental, and we
have to take credit for it.

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:04.803
I do it sometimes, surely
out of embarrassment.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:07.940
So I believe the true path to success

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:09.990
is through the fear of failure.

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:12.240
If you aren't scared enough of failing,

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:14.190
you're unlikely to succeed.

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:16.180
It's not pleasant to fail at stuff.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:17.380
All of us experienced it.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:19.760
You will too, if you haven't already.

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:21.750
So use it to succeed.

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:24.720
How I've done is a few
points I'd like to say,

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:27.050
and then I'll end this monologue,

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:28.680
firstly it's not the absence of failure

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:32.020
that makes you success,
that makes success.

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:34.550
It is your response to
failure that actually helps

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:36.860
to buffer the reverses
that you experienced.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:40.770
I personally have one response
to failure, pragmatism.

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:42.610
A recognition and believe
that if one approach

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:45.693
does not work, then the
other will or might.

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:48.130
Failure also gives me an incentive

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:51.230
to greater exertion, harder work,

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:55.193
which invariably leads to a
later success in most cases.

00:28:55.193 --> 00:28:57.250
Repeated failure has taught me

00:28:57.250 --> 00:28:59.530
to stop pretending I'm someone else.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:00.910
It's given me the clarity to stick

00:29:00.910 --> 00:29:02.210
to things that really matter to me

00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:04.983
instead of distracting me from my core.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:08.820
Failure also gets you to find
who your real friends are.

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.390
The true strength of your relationships

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:13.393
only gets tested in the
face of strong adversity.

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:16.860
Overcoming some of my failures has made me

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:18.881
discover that I have a strong will

00:29:18.881 --> 00:29:21.600
and more discipline than I suspected.

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:22.990
It has helped me to have confidence

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:25.283
in my ability to survive.

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:27.440
Failure is an amazing teacher.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:29.440
There's a well known story
of this very successful man

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:31.730
and a reporter asked him, sir, how was it

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:36.410
that you always succeed, then
he said, right decisions.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:37.760
And he said, how do you
make right decisions?

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:40.519
And he says, you know, experience.

00:29:40.519 --> 00:29:42.930
Yes, yes, how do you get so experienced?

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:45.268
It's wrong decisions and failure.

00:29:45.268 --> 00:29:46.460
(audience laughing)

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:48.973
So do fail, and it's alright to fail.

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:51.230
You have to know and learn that life

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:53.750
is not just a checklist of acquisitions,

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:56.350
attainments, and fulfillments.

00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:59.743
Your qualifications and
CVs don't really matter.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.540
Instead life is difficult and complicated.

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:05.900
And beyond anyone's control.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:08.410
The humility to know it will help you

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:10.870
survive its vicissitudes.

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:13.721
This is the second big
word that I've learned.

00:30:13.721 --> 00:30:17.971
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:20.980
But I don't want to sound dark.

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:22.540
My hope for all of you is that you retain

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:25.100
a lifelong love of
learning, that you never

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:27.950
cease to dream exciting
and inspiring dreams,

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.090
and when you fail, you
don't, and you fail,

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.910
you fail well enough to
succeed the next time.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.270
Don't be afraid of being afraid.

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:37.740
Be afraid of not facing
your fears and failures.

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.280
As I was coming here,
and this is my dream,

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.980
I was telling the press
outside when I walked in here,

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:45.960
I sent a message to my little daughter

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:47.870
who's 11 and a half, my son 14,

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:49.450
and I said it's one of the most beautiful

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:51.460
institutions I've seen in my life.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.537
Genuinely, you guys are
fortunate to be studying here.

00:30:54.537 --> 00:30:57.701
(audience applauding)

00:30:57.701 --> 00:31:00.530
And I really wish my kids
are good enough, like you,

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:02.590
all you young boys and
girls and all the parents

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:04.830
who've helped them do this,
are good enough to reach here

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:06.890
and study and get this opportunity.

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:08.150
So I wrote to my daughter, and said,

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:09.470
so pretty and nice and beautiful,

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:10.650
I wish you could come and study here,

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.780
and she sent me back a long
message as she always does.

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:14.900
Is it pretty, Papa?

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:15.733
Is it nice?

00:31:15.733 --> 00:31:16.750
Is it snowing there?

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:19.440
Because I told her last time
I was here it was snowing.

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:21.973
And my son sent me one alphabet.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:26.380
K.
(audience laughing)

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:29.893
Which I have understood
when you extend means OK.

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:33.050
And for some reason he's also written,

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:35.090
Papa, Chuck Norris has trained his dog

00:31:35.090 --> 00:31:39.409
to pick up its own poo.
(audience laughing)

00:31:39.409 --> 00:31:43.143
Because Chuck Norris will
not take shit from anyone.

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:48.590
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.930
Remember all boys and girls, that you're

00:31:56.930 --> 00:32:01.036
fortunate enough to be funambulists,

00:32:01.036 --> 00:32:03.480
you have an amazing set of punching bags,

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:05.860
your parents cherish them always,

00:32:05.860 --> 00:32:09.250
and failure is your fiendish
friend, keep him close.

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:10.650
And as my son sent a message,

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:12.623
don't take no shit from anybody.

00:32:13.582 --> 00:32:14.653
(audience cheering)
God bless you.

00:32:15.611 --> 00:32:20.611
Thank you very much.
(audience applauding)

00:32:21.035 --> 00:32:22.411
Thank you, you're all very kind,

00:32:22.411 --> 00:32:24.514
and I wish you all the best in life.

00:32:24.514 --> 00:32:26.738
Thank you very much.

00:32:26.738 --> 00:32:27.571
Thank you.

00:32:30.045 --> 00:32:31.712
Can I come back now?

00:32:32.963 --> 00:32:35.963
(audience cheering)

00:33:12.075 --> 00:33:13.013
- You're right here.
- Oh am I?

00:33:13.013 --> 00:33:14.713
- Yeah.
- Are you sure?

00:33:14.713 --> 00:33:17.713
(audience cheering)

00:33:20.396 --> 00:33:23.634
- I love you!
- I love you, Khan!

00:33:23.634 --> 00:33:26.634
(audience cheering)

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:31.433
(guests laughing)

00:33:35.311 --> 00:33:36.840
- Hello everyone again.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:38.470
I've discovered, I've discovered

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:40.010
that if ever become a movie star,

00:33:40.010 --> 00:33:42.889
you can't see a thing up here.
- You can't.

00:33:42.889 --> 00:33:44.525
- Yeah no, not a thing.

00:33:44.525 --> 00:33:47.112
My name is Jeff Brenzel,
I'm the dean of admissions

00:33:47.112 --> 00:33:49.452
here at Yale, these are my people.

00:33:49.452 --> 00:33:50.940
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:53.090
I'm the master of Timothy Dwight College,

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:55.878
a lecture in the department of philosophy

00:33:55.878 --> 00:33:57.270
and I keep the flame
for the Chubb Fellowship

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:59.410
which I'm incredibly
happy to say has brought

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:03.064
SRK to New Haven.
(audience cheering)

00:34:03.064 --> 00:34:04.603
So I just wanna tell him right now

00:34:04.603 --> 00:34:06.436
in front of God and everybody here

00:34:06.436 --> 00:34:08.730
that I think his chances
for admission here to Yale

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:12.393
would be pretty good.
(audience cheering)

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:15.750
And we're hoping he's gonna have time

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:17.360
to drop by the office to fill out

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:19.253
the application before he leaves.

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:23.940
So here with me to ask a few questions

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:26.560
is Nikhil Sud who is born and brought up

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:30.060
in New Delhi, India, and
attended St. Columba's School,

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:32.190
which was SRK's school.

00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:34.860
He then came to Yale where
he received his Bachelor's

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:39.453
in economics in 2010,
economics, just like SRK.

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:42.493
It gets more eerie as you go.

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:48.152
While at Yale, actually he
departed from Shah Rukh's

00:34:48.152 --> 00:34:52.140
career and he founded Yale Hindi Debate,

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:54.690
a national level competition
in the United States

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:57.730
that takes place entirely
in the Hindi language,

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:00.010
and it's now in its fifth year.

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:03.089
He's currently a student
at the Yale Law School.

00:35:03.089 --> 00:35:06.140
(audience applauding)

00:35:06.140 --> 00:35:07.560
We're going for a dual language

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:09.240
country here sooner or later.

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:11.850
While he hasn't yet charted
out his career plans,

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:13.790
he did tell me that he's got one goal,

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:16.843
to get Mr. Shah Rukh Khan
to follow him on Twitter.

00:35:17.738 --> 00:35:20.112
(audience cheering)

00:35:20.112 --> 00:35:22.270
- No.
- Not yet.

00:35:22.270 --> 00:35:24.730
He hasn't achieved this goal yet.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:26.820
Then we have Sarika Arya who graduated

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:29.960
from Yale College in
2011 with a double major

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:31.980
in ethics, politics, and economics,

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:33.773
and also theater studies.

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:37.710
Her parents emigrated
from India to America,

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:39.570
where she and her brother
were born and raised

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:42.400
until they moved to
London for several years,

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:45.610
while here at Yale, Sarika
studied international affairs,

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:47.990
human rights activism, and the use

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:51.290
of theater to impact social change.

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:53.770
She's currently working as
a Woodbridge Hall Fellow

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:56.380
at the Yale Office of
International Affairs.

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:59.903
Next year she's off to
London School of Economics.

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:03.561
So please welcome the guests.

00:36:03.561 --> 00:36:06.728
(audience applauding)

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.330
We have some questions,
everyone had their own

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:12.920
particular question that
they wanted to ask Shah Rukh,

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:15.320
and we're gonna start with Nikhil.

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:16.293
- Hello, Mr. Khan.

00:36:17.210 --> 00:36:19.630
I felt you spoke really truly inspiringly

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:23.410
about valuing creativity,
and about facing your fears.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:25.840
My concern is, I feel there
are many students out there

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.750
who are passionate about creativity

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:29.810
and are also generally brave people

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:32.470
who can face their fears,
but in spite of this,

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:35.360
they choose safer, more
traditional, non-creative

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.840
paths in life, because
they feel the creative path

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:40.710
is simply too risky no
matter how brave they are.

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:43.230
And even though they love
creativity for its own sake,

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:45.290
not for buttering it for money,

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:47.351
they feel they still need to be able

00:36:47.351 --> 00:36:49.060
to pay for the bills and
that might not happen.

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:51.800
So for a person who is devoted to the arts

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:54.450
but due to certain practical
realities of the world,

00:36:54.450 --> 00:36:57.080
selects a non-creative
path, is it possible

00:36:57.080 --> 00:37:00.370
for such a person to lead a
truly happy, fulfilling life?

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:02.120
On a personal note, I'm really worried,

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:04.650
because I'm a huge fan
of creative writing.

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.910
And I did a lot of that
in school and at college,

00:37:06.910 --> 00:37:08.650
but now I've chosen law school.

00:37:08.650 --> 00:37:10.140
And I console myself by saying,

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:12.950
so what if you're not
writing movies or TV shows,

00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:15.678
you'll still be writing
legal briefs and legal memos,

00:37:15.678 --> 00:37:16.511
(audience laughing)
and I think,

00:37:17.391 --> 00:37:18.480
you know, while on some
level, that makes sense,

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.730
I think it's an extremely
depressing thought.

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.810
And I do feel like somebody's
stepping on my oxygen tube,

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.760
but I fear that the alternative

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:28.580
might feel like somebody simply cut off

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:31.650
my oxygen tube because I
won't be able to pay my bills.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:34.560
So my question really is, and I ask this

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:37.240
on behalf of everyone
like me, can I be happy?

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:38.073
And if so how?

00:37:39.142 --> 00:37:43.470
(everyone laughing and applauding)

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:46.480
- An easy one.
- An easy one, yeah, yeah.

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:48.280
Nikhil, let me tell you,
you've got problems, dude.

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:51.463
- [Nikhil] I know.
(audience laughing)

00:37:53.404 --> 00:37:56.190
- I tell you how I've done it.

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:59.270
I did theater, and I truly believe

00:37:59.270 --> 00:38:01.663
in being very creative, very artistic,

00:38:02.668 --> 00:38:03.930
and a very serious actor at times.

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:04.763
I really do.

00:38:05.946 --> 00:38:08.130
When I see a lot of work
that is being done around

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:10.900
by other actors in my
own country and outside,

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:14.500
I feel envious at times that
I've not been able to do that.

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:16.110
Because like you said, when I came on

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:18.550
to Mumbai, and I will
say this to all the kids,

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:20.450
that yes, surviving is the most,

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:21.410
that's what the whole speech was,

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:23.630
that you have, I'll
teach you how to survive.

00:38:23.630 --> 00:38:26.468
I'm not gonna give you any thoughts about,

00:38:26.468 --> 00:38:29.840
that, listen it's alright
to go on (mumbles)

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:31.350
and do your writing, creative writing,

00:38:31.350 --> 00:38:32.500
whether anyone reads it or not.

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:34.170
No, it's important that people read it.

00:38:34.170 --> 00:38:36.050
It's important that it
becomes a best seller.

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:38.140
As a matter fact, I very shamelessly say

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:40.640
I am the most capitalist human being

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:43.000
that you will ever find
on the face of this Earth.

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:45.800
I truly believe that
you need to find a place

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:48.640
where you can make the
choices that you want to make.

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:51.410
You cannot just say,
you know, I had a friend

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:54.090
who's a creative writer,
and who fortunately,

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:57.810
still is not a lawyer, and,
(audience laughing)

00:38:57.810 --> 00:39:00.030
he wanted to write books,
and he's working here

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:01.840
in America, in New York actually.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:03.730
And he asked me own day, his father died,

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:05.230
and he said, you know I wanna write books.

00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:06.180
I wanna write creative stuff

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:07.420
on spirituality and goodness,

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:09.280
and I don't want to write anything else.

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:10.113
How am I gonna survive?

00:39:10.113 --> 00:39:11.890
My father's dead, my
mother's not being able

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:12.970
to look after me, and I said,

00:39:12.970 --> 00:39:17.940
you go down, and start working
in advertising company,

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.735
where you started writing, I do remember,

00:39:20.735 --> 00:39:22.480
you know we, this is a
joke we had in college,

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:24.230
I don't know if it's still here.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:28.080
It was an innerwear ad, with,

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:29.950
like a Calvin Klein underwear model

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:31.910
and you write, it's for everyone,

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:33.260
every Tom, Dick, and Harry.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:39.607
Which, if you write this
stuff, you write it,

00:39:39.607 --> 00:39:41.960
and earn the money, pay your bills,

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:44.190
don't let them cut your oxygen tube out.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:46.970
But, after you are in
that position of choice,

00:39:46.970 --> 00:39:49.970
don't misuse it, and that's the one thing

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:51.580
that I was, I decided to do.

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:53.870
I do lot of advertising, lot of people,

00:39:53.870 --> 00:39:57.910
question me on the uncreative
bits of work that I do.

00:39:57.910 --> 00:40:00.700
I do, I think out of 10 things that I do,

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:03.240
I think seven of them
are highly uncreative.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:04.383
Highly uncreative.

00:40:05.290 --> 00:40:06.980
And very disturbing at times.

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:09.110
I have a big bathroom now.

00:40:09.110 --> 00:40:11.540
I sit there and cry after
having done those things.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:13.440
I go, I generally do, I'm not lying.

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:15.490
I want kids to know this,
that yeah, I do that,

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:17.380
I get, I'm like I don't wanna do this,

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:19.100
but I take the money from that,

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:21.290
and I would like to be able to create

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:24.620
a production house, a
filmmaking institution,

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:25.890
which would do the creative stuff

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.020
which I wanted to do when I was 25.

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:30.270
It may get a little late, it may happen

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:32.350
when I'm 50 or 55, may not have happened

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:34.990
when I dreamed of it at 26, 27.

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:37.160
But I'm sure that I will
make the right choices.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.920
So please, all you
kids, do not get rallied

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:41.690
by anyone who tells you, it's wonderful

00:40:41.690 --> 00:40:44.620
to be creative and poor and hungry, no.

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:46.700
You can't be that, you have to survive,

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.990
you owe it to your parents,
you owe to your life.

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.150
Be well, have good cars,
have a good television,

00:40:51.150 --> 00:40:53.070
have three PlayStations if you need,

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:56.170
but when you get into
that position of choice,

00:40:56.170 --> 00:40:58.500
make sure you don't
cheat with your choices.

00:40:58.500 --> 00:40:59.333
So that's one thing.

00:40:59.333 --> 00:41:00.683
So do your legal briefs.

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:05.210
And on the side, when you
have become this really

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:09.211
famous wonderful lawyer,
saving convicts and,

00:41:09.211 --> 00:41:13.690
(audience laughing)
and criminals,

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:16.160
go into that bathroom,
cry a bit that you saved

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:18.540
another criminal today, but with the money

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:20.433
that you earn from that mafia Don,

00:41:23.264 --> 00:41:28.264
write, write a good story.
(audience cheering)

00:41:29.866 --> 00:41:31.280
- Alright, Sarika.
- Alright.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:34.580
- So India's a country
of hundreds of languages,

00:41:34.580 --> 00:41:37.240
cultures, religions, customs, and so on.

00:41:37.240 --> 00:41:40.590
And as a result, the country
grapples with divisiveness

00:41:40.590 --> 00:41:42.930
on many social issues, but your films

00:41:42.930 --> 00:41:47.930
have created a truly
devoted and united fan base

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:51.770
that's united in their love
of you, Shah Rukh Khan.

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:56.102
And they're,
(audience cheering)

00:41:56.102 --> 00:41:58.920
they're united across ethnic
and national boundaries,

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.390
even outside of India,
yet at the same time,

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:03.790
some of your films do
deal with social issues.

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:08.030
So, My Name is Khan, it
confronted racial profiling

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:11.820
after 9/11, and Chak De! India discussed

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:13.460
bigotry and sexism.

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:15.883
- Those are the stories that I write.

00:42:17.925 --> 00:42:18.950
- There you go.

00:42:18.950 --> 00:42:21.150
But at the same time,
you also do have films

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:23.310
that are sheer entertainment.

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:26.830
So what is it about your
work that appeals to

00:42:26.830 --> 00:42:30.024
and unites audiences
that are normally at odds

00:42:30.024 --> 00:42:32.820
with each other, and also, do you feel

00:42:32.820 --> 00:42:35.826
that films have, or
maybe they already have,

00:42:35.826 --> 00:42:40.503
a role in discussing or
examining social issues?

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:43.380
- I mean, I think they have.

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:45.960
But if they make it their prime concern,

00:42:45.960 --> 00:42:47.210
then they don't remain film,

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:48.680
they don't remain entertainment.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:51.463
I say this to everyone,
it's a little saying,

00:42:51.463 --> 00:42:53.640
that messages are for the
postbox, post offices,

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:55.200
they're not for entertainment.

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:56.033
You don't give messages
through entertainment.

00:42:56.033 --> 00:42:57.730
I'm a big believer of that.

00:42:57.730 --> 00:42:59.960
You need to entertain
people, two and a half hour

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:02.760
in dark room, come back,
have fun, enjoy yourself,

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:04.610
and entertainment can be
sad, it can be morbid,

00:43:04.610 --> 00:43:06.400
it can be bright, it can be colorful,

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:09.030
it can be as silly as some
of the films that I do.

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:11.580
But I truly believe that
the reason, I think,

00:43:11.580 --> 00:43:13.310
I was analyzing it, I keep analyzing it

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:14.930
because everyone keeps asking me

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:16.970
how did this happen that
people everywhere like you,

00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:19.130
and I cannot go beyond the answer

00:43:19.130 --> 00:43:20.999
that it has to do with my dimples.

00:43:20.999 --> 00:43:25.952
(Jeffrey laughing)
(audience cheering)

00:43:25.952 --> 00:43:27.610
It's the most intellectual answer

00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:29.852
I've been able to give myself.

00:43:29.852 --> 00:43:31.202
- I understand.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:43:31.202 --> 00:43:33.288
- That's it.
- Are you good?

00:43:33.288 --> 00:43:36.314
And you see how far you
go with these dimples.

00:43:36.314 --> 00:43:37.193
- [Sarika] I'll keep you posted.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:41.664
- But I think the reason that my cinema,

00:43:41.664 --> 00:43:43.510
or the kind of films, I think
I got a little fortunate

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:46.440
that in the '90s when I started working,

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:49.460
there was a time when, '80s was the time

00:43:49.460 --> 00:43:51.700
of a lot of aggressive films

00:43:51.700 --> 00:43:54.190
and ugly bell bottoms and big collars

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:56.570
and strange hairstyles in Indian cinema,

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:59.390
and somehow the younger generation,

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:02.589
which was kind of me also
that time I was 24, 23,

00:44:02.589 --> 00:44:07.300
they all wanted to have a
little more love amidst them,

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:11.220
not anti-establishment
work, not anti-social work,

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:13.340
they just wanted to have feeling of love.

00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:15.800
And I think I did a
lot of films like that.

00:44:15.800 --> 00:44:17.940
So whether it was Dilwale
Dulhania Le Jayenge,

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:21.063
or,
(audience cheering)

00:44:22.208 --> 00:44:26.470
or Kuch Kuch Hota Hai.
(audience cheering)

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:29.600
And I think as cliched as it may sound,

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:31.757
but it makes me believe that love

00:44:31.757 --> 00:44:33.440
really is the universal language.

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:35.600
So you can be in a country
with 100 difficult languages

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:38.880
as you said, and I come
down to Yale, to America,

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:40.890
to London, I go to South America,

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:43.870
and people in Germany who don't
speak our language at all,

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:47.820
and you know, they all
seem to like that cinema.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:50.460
I asked one lady, a 60
year old lady who actually

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.030
took the first Indian film to Germany.

00:44:52.030 --> 00:44:54.210
She used to work for a
big television studio

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:56.937
and I asked her, I was
sitting with her at night,

00:44:56.937 --> 00:44:58.450
and I said, why do you wanna do Hindi,

00:44:58.450 --> 00:44:59.630
why do you guys like Hindi films?

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:02.479
I go and shoot in
Germany, and they're like

00:45:02.479 --> 00:45:04.030
hundreds of Germans who love our films.

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:06.029
And they don't speak Hindi at all.

00:45:06.029 --> 00:45:09.310
They just say, achtung, achtung,
achtung, no Hindi at all.

00:45:09.310 --> 00:45:10.143
Nothing.

00:45:11.155 --> 00:45:14.181
They make these guttural sounds
but they don't speak Hindi.

00:45:14.181 --> 00:45:15.392
(audience laughing)

00:45:15.392 --> 00:45:16.370
And you know, they look very imposing,

00:45:16.370 --> 00:45:18.090
because from the outside, we all have

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:19.890
preset notions of each country

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:21.480
and country, people from there.

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:24.930
And I asked them, I said,
why do you like Hindi films?

00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:26.373
Why do you like this kind of cinema?

00:45:26.373 --> 00:45:30.000
They told me that I have
a button to drive a car.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.960
I push on the bottom
(imitates a revving sound),

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:32.793
the car starts.

00:45:34.004 --> 00:45:35.160
I have a button to make coffee.

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:37.890
I have a button to go up and down.

00:45:37.890 --> 00:45:41.033
In Germany, we didn't
have a button to cry.

00:45:42.070 --> 00:45:45.913
And you give us that button,
your films give us that button.

00:45:47.218 --> 00:45:50.540
And I think, you know, and
so I equate these two things,

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:55.350
I think love, as beautiful
as it is, also makes you cry.

00:45:55.350 --> 00:45:58.865
And in a good way, sometimes in a bad way.

00:45:58.865 --> 00:45:59.860
(audience laughing)

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:02.340
But I think the togetherness of this,

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:04.670
I think the whole human community

00:46:04.670 --> 00:46:08.350
around the world, you'll
find two universal truths,

00:46:08.350 --> 00:46:10.530
they all love and they all cry.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:13.830
And I think if your work, if your writing,

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:18.830
if your job can deal with
that without knowing it,

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:20.590
you'll be bringing social changes.

00:46:20.590 --> 00:46:23.280
You don't need to make
an attempt, I think,

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:24.787
you just need to say the truth and say,

00:46:24.787 --> 00:46:27.900
there's a little about
love, for a father-son,

00:46:27.900 --> 00:46:29.730
for a father-daughter, for a man-woman,

00:46:29.730 --> 00:46:32.350
for a mother-son, for a teacher-student,

00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:33.830
it's a little about crying.

00:46:33.830 --> 00:46:35.240
And when you write stories like that

00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:37.880
or show stories like that
or incidents like that,

00:46:37.880 --> 00:46:39.928
I think it just unites us because we all

00:46:39.928 --> 00:46:42.503
do go into that bathroom and cry.

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:44.720
So we all have that bathroom and I think

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:46.830
we all feel like loving, so I think yeah,

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:49.430
I think if you keep it as
simple as loving and crying,

00:46:49.430 --> 00:46:51.390
each cinema, each theater, everything

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:53.323
will have its social impact.

00:46:55.028 --> 00:46:59.945
- [Sarika] Thank you.
(audience applauding)

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:06.965
- The man who gave the
Germans permission to cry.

00:47:06.965 --> 00:47:08.930
(audience laughing)

00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:09.763
Alive.

00:47:11.271 --> 00:47:12.723
That's fantastic.

00:47:13.900 --> 00:47:16.690
Shah Rukh, honestly, if it
weren't for my good friends here,

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:19.500
so many South Asian students
and faculty here at Yale,

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:21.530
I wouldn't know your films.

00:47:21.530 --> 00:47:24.629
South Asian Film Society
has been spreading

00:47:24.629 --> 00:47:28.940
the gospel here, a lot of
people are becoming attracted

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:32.020
to the cinema, but of
course I came not as these

00:47:32.020 --> 00:47:36.240
two students but as an
outsider to Indian cinema.

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:38.253
I'd heard of something called Bollywood,

00:47:38.253 --> 00:47:41.810
and I fell into the
trap that many of us do

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:43.353
of thinking that actually meant something,

00:47:43.353 --> 00:47:47.110
that it was, in some kind
of relation to Hollywood.

00:47:47.110 --> 00:47:50.868
And then when I first
started watching the films,

00:47:50.868 --> 00:47:55.300
I realize, there's no, there's
no Hollywood take off here,

00:47:55.300 --> 00:47:57.588
that's not what's going on.

00:47:57.588 --> 00:48:01.420
They, the films were like
nothing I'd ever seen.

00:48:01.420 --> 00:48:03.840
They had their own language,
they had their own world,

00:48:03.840 --> 00:48:05.740
they had their own way of seeing things.

00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:09.570
Mostly, I have to say this
incredibly enthusiasm for life.

00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:12.390
They were fantastically original.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:15.260
Which was just a startling experience.

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:17.350
I didn't know of the
existence of this world.

00:48:17.350 --> 00:48:20.890
So I wanted to ask you,
what do you see happening

00:48:20.890 --> 00:48:25.290
as Bollywood, as Indian
film, as Indian cinema,

00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:29.002
you're always doing something new, but,

00:48:29.002 --> 00:48:32.550
that influence is expanding, is moving out

00:48:32.550 --> 00:48:36.600
into the world in some
very remarkable ways.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:41.077
Is Indian movie making,
influencing Western movie making?

00:48:41.077 --> 00:48:45.150
Is Western movie making
creeping into Indian cinema?

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:46.730
Are these ships passing in the night

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:48.210
or are they talking to each other?

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:52.438
I'm dying to know what
you think about this.

00:48:52.438 --> 00:48:53.888
- Yeah, I think, you know what,

00:48:53.888 --> 00:48:56.790
our cinemas are very unique and I remember

00:48:56.790 --> 00:48:59.070
hearing Quentin Tarantino at Cannes,

00:48:59.070 --> 00:49:02.420
and he said the only reason,
and I just wanna point out,

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:04.890
that it's the only film
industry in the world

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:06.913
which has survived Hollywood.
- Yes.

00:49:07.965 --> 00:49:09.637
- Yeah, all the film industries,

00:49:09.637 --> 00:49:10.700
whether it's Japan, or
any place in Europe,

00:49:10.700 --> 00:49:13.280
the local cinema kind
of has been taken over

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:15.000
by the more popular commercial,

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.710
wonderful Hollywood cinema
that is made, I'm a big fan.

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:19.408
India has survived that.

00:49:19.408 --> 00:49:20.241
We still make the largest number of films,

00:49:20.241 --> 00:49:25.241
and the reason for that is
that we still believe in stars.

00:49:25.260 --> 00:49:28.450
We believe that they're
people who can lead us.

00:49:28.450 --> 00:49:31.760
We don't think a movie star
is just a professional.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:33.590
We look up to him or her.

00:49:33.590 --> 00:49:35.900
We think of him or her as role models.

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:37.950
I'm the wrong one, don't ever do that, I,

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:41.561
I've been hiding in the
gully and smoking and stuff.

00:49:41.561 --> 00:49:43.590
(audience laughing)
I'm a bad boy.

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:48.590
But the whole system of
looking up to someone,

00:49:48.830 --> 00:49:52.380
or trying to find a hero, has
made Indian cinema survive.

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:54.530
Having said that, our
stories are very unique,

00:49:54.530 --> 00:49:55.920
very culturally (mumbles) and of course

00:49:55.920 --> 00:49:57.960
we have this uniqueness
of what used to happen

00:49:57.960 --> 00:50:00.180
in the '60s in Hollywood,
we have musicals.

00:50:00.180 --> 00:50:02.240
We break into a song and dance any time.

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:06.560
So, yeah, yeah, I, he
was saying it so kindly.

00:50:06.560 --> 00:50:09.560
(audience cheering)

00:50:13.287 --> 00:50:18.243
He was being so wonderful and
said, I was startled by them.

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:21.613
It's bloody shocking.
- I was.

00:50:21.613 --> 00:50:23.830
- What we do on screen, yes, I know.

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:27.420
Like (gasps), why are they dancing?

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:29.957
We do that, and actually, our films

00:50:29.957 --> 00:50:32.370
are like a variety show, a cabaret.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:35.479
Because, like she said,
we have hundred languages,

00:50:35.479 --> 00:50:38.140
we still live in one family households.

00:50:38.140 --> 00:50:40.520
So the grandmom's there,
the grandchild's there,

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:42.220
the great grandchild is there,

00:50:42.220 --> 00:50:44.810
and all of us watching cinema is the only

00:50:44.810 --> 00:50:46.930
escape route we have in our country.

00:50:46.930 --> 00:50:49.600
We don't have skiing slopes,
we don't have car racing,

00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:51.100
we've got a little bit
of sports coming in,

00:50:51.100 --> 00:50:53.120
our television just
started, so what happens

00:50:53.120 --> 00:50:54.984
is you need to have that, hey,

00:50:54.984 --> 00:50:57.445
listen, I'm not bothered about life,

00:50:57.445 --> 00:50:58.870
for this two and a half hour
I'm going to be overrode

00:50:58.870 --> 00:51:01.382
by this fun and the grandmom says

00:51:01.382 --> 00:51:03.390
I want to hear actually
something spiritual.

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:04.483
So we put spiritual.

00:51:05.556 --> 00:51:07.900
And she says, I wanna see this

00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:09.210
cool guy with dimples dancing.

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:11.750
So okay cool guy.
(audience laughing)

00:51:11.750 --> 00:51:13.290
And you say, I need to be startled,

00:51:13.290 --> 00:51:15.550
so here, startling you.

00:51:15.550 --> 00:51:18.150
So we put everything in it.

00:51:18.150 --> 00:51:22.110
And unabashedly so, and
happily so, and celebrate life

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:26.170
because life, I think, has all of it,

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:28.283
life has never been that, you don't sing.

00:51:29.330 --> 00:51:30.397
You sing while shaving and (mumbles),

00:51:31.232 --> 00:51:34.290
and there's no isolation
of emotions in real life.

00:51:34.290 --> 00:51:35.850
Everything is happening together.

00:51:35.850 --> 00:51:37.780
And it's wonderful when
I see Western cinema

00:51:37.780 --> 00:51:39.810
that isolates emotion,
there's more linear,

00:51:39.810 --> 00:51:41.030
there's more story telling, there's more

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:43.610
character-driven stuff,
but I think our cinema,

00:51:43.610 --> 00:51:46.100
because it's just
celebration and celebrator,

00:51:46.100 --> 00:51:48.523
it makes you feel a
little attracted to it.

00:51:49.411 --> 00:51:51.762
You know, you say, okay,
let it be, no, let's move,

00:51:51.762 --> 00:51:52.723
let's sit down and
watch this and have fun.

00:51:53.561 --> 00:51:55.610
A lot of technology from Hollywood,

00:51:55.610 --> 00:51:58.440
people like myself and the
younger actors and directors

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:00.636
want to take it on, because
I think technically,

00:52:00.636 --> 00:52:03.667
the Western cinema's about
40 to 50 years ahead of us,

00:52:03.667 --> 00:52:05.630
and we need to bring it to India.

00:52:05.630 --> 00:52:07.510
That's the only way we'll
be able to come down,

00:52:07.510 --> 00:52:09.080
come up in the international market.

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:14.080
We are about 0.02% of world
market as far as economics go.

00:52:14.350 --> 00:52:16.060
We did make the maximum number of films,

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:19.120
but in terms of economics,
we are very small.

00:52:19.120 --> 00:52:20.840
The only way we can do that is not change

00:52:20.840 --> 00:52:22.910
the language of our films, the technology,

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:25.010
and the technique of story telling.

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:29.800
We do it in a, we have,
we do films in three acts.

00:52:29.800 --> 00:52:31.628
We have an interval.

00:52:31.628 --> 00:52:33.330
And I was, I have to
tell you this incident,

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:36.103
I made a film call Asoka which was about

00:52:36.103 --> 00:52:39.550
(mumbles) Buddha, it's
a great film, I thought,

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:42.380
and unfortunately, sorry
to have to mention it here,

00:52:42.380 --> 00:52:44.690
it released on 9/11 so it kind of tanked,

00:52:44.690 --> 00:52:47.710
didn't do well and I was showing it

00:52:47.710 --> 00:52:51.233
to an English set of audience
just before I came here,

00:52:51.233 --> 00:52:55.060
and the film went on and
on, and it's really long,

00:52:55.060 --> 00:52:56.560
we make three hour long films.

00:52:57.580 --> 00:52:59.890
Before they all entered, I
had all these British people

00:52:59.890 --> 00:53:02.033
saying, why do you guys have interval?

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:05.340
What does it mean?

00:53:05.340 --> 00:53:08.218
Why should we have break interval here?

00:53:08.218 --> 00:53:11.307
It breaks the whole
sequencing of a story tell.

00:53:11.307 --> 00:53:13.034
I said I'm sorry, but we have an interval,

00:53:13.034 --> 00:53:14.300
after one and a half hours, they were like

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:17.013
holding my hand, why don't you
guys have the interval now?

00:53:18.081 --> 00:53:19.671
(audience laughing)

00:53:19.671 --> 00:53:23.560
So our cinema looks after
everybody's wants and desires,

00:53:23.560 --> 00:53:26.070
as in when they crop up, but we,

00:53:27.828 --> 00:53:32.828
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.250
but yeah, there is a whole mix together.

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:37.340
I think technically, if we do better,

00:53:37.340 --> 00:53:39.390
we'll have more world cinema audience

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:42.160
going watching it, and story wise

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:46.743
I think our stories are
about hope and simple hopes.

00:53:46.743 --> 00:53:48.570
And they're not too outlandish,

00:53:48.570 --> 00:53:49.900
though it seems so sometimes.

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:52.530
Everyone tells me Indian
cinema's unrealistic.

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.100
But it's as realistic as the president

00:53:54.100 --> 00:53:55.840
of the United States
sitting in Air Force One

00:53:55.840 --> 00:53:57.640
and going and destroying the meteor.

00:53:59.542 --> 00:54:02.692
Wow, yeah, it can happen.

00:54:02.692 --> 00:54:06.060
As unrealistic, and I think
the story should remain

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:09.630
culturally bound in
terms of the simple hopes

00:54:09.630 --> 00:54:12.720
and simple ideologies,
while the technology,

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:13.800
I think we should start taking

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:15.420
and we are already from Hollywood.

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:17.530
Lot of studios have
now opened up in India,

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:18.940
and there's this huge give and take

00:54:18.940 --> 00:54:21.548
and it's fantastic for
world cinema I think.

00:54:21.548 --> 00:54:23.560
I'm here in America and I'm not doing it

00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:26.050
because I'm here, but I
genuinely want to thank

00:54:26.050 --> 00:54:28.640
all the studios in
Hollywood, because I've been

00:54:28.640 --> 00:54:30.240
interacting with them in
the last five, six years.

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:32.880
My Name is Khan was actually made by Fox,

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:34.730
and they were kind enough to release it

00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:36.870
as we would really like it
to be shown to everyone.

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:39.890
So there's this huge
synergies that are happening,

00:54:39.890 --> 00:54:42.250
and I think yeah, Indian
cinema's really gaining from it

00:54:42.250 --> 00:54:44.020
and hopefully one day you'll not

00:54:44.020 --> 00:54:46.130
be so startled by our films.

00:54:46.130 --> 00:54:50.380
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:56.350
- So just some advice for
my fellow White people

00:54:56.350 --> 00:54:58.900
who are not Germans in the audience,

00:54:58.900 --> 00:55:02.400
because I know that you haven't
watched enough of his films.

00:55:02.400 --> 00:55:05.290
This is something to go
home now and do and I hope

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:09.000
it inspires you that you
heard a little bit about it.

00:55:09.000 --> 00:55:12.240
You will want to relax,
you'll want to have

00:55:12.240 --> 00:55:15.230
a very comfortable couch,
you'll wanna be surrounded

00:55:15.230 --> 00:55:17.794
by some nice food and
some family and friends.

00:55:17.794 --> 00:55:19.850
- And a grandmom.
- And a grandmom.

00:55:19.850 --> 00:55:23.850
Grandmom should come visit
while you watch Indian films.

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:25.283
Nikhil, another question?

00:55:26.182 --> 00:55:28.410
- Okay, so you've achieved so much

00:55:28.410 --> 00:55:29.850
in so many different fields.

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:33.480
You're globally successful
and adored actor and producer,

00:55:33.480 --> 00:55:35.650
you own a cricket team,
you're a philanthropist,

00:55:35.650 --> 00:55:38.650
and I think you're a real
role model for all of us.

00:55:38.650 --> 00:55:40.700
My question is, what next?

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:42.360
And I know that you
maintain that you always

00:55:42.360 --> 00:55:44.810
wanna keep making people
smile, but is there

00:55:44.810 --> 00:55:46.690
a specific kind of film or project

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:49.120
that you really want to
do and haven't yet done?

00:55:49.120 --> 00:55:51.520
I suppose what I'm asking
is, what is the biggest,

00:55:51.520 --> 00:55:54.110
most exciting, most motivating thing

00:55:54.110 --> 00:55:56.920
on your to do list if you have one?

00:55:56.920 --> 00:55:59.360
- No, Nikhil, I've been, I'm sorry,

00:55:59.360 --> 00:56:02.160
I've been just, like I
said in the beginning,

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:05.560
that I just work towards my
dreams and I just keep running,

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:06.640
I don't know where I'm gonna stop,

00:56:06.640 --> 00:56:08.140
and things happen along the way.

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:10.120
I started wanting to be a soccer player.

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:12.190
I did economics, I did
mass communications,

00:56:12.190 --> 00:56:14.660
my Master's in that, and everyone thought

00:56:14.660 --> 00:56:17.153
I'll do something intelligent
but I became an actor.

00:56:18.419 --> 00:56:20.870
I came to Mumbai for a year
because my parents died.

00:56:20.870 --> 00:56:23.060
I told my wife, I said,
we just go for a year,

00:56:23.060 --> 00:56:25.840
come back, I told all my friends
that I'll be back in a year

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:26.880
because they kick me out of this,

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:28.743
21 years and I'm still there.

00:56:29.641 --> 00:56:30.970
So things just happen and I realize

00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:33.970
the core is really, and my
job, and as an entertainer,

00:56:33.970 --> 00:56:35.870
and people may look down upon it at times,

00:56:35.870 --> 00:56:37.420
people will be happy with it, some people

00:56:37.420 --> 00:56:40.370
may not appreciate it,
but I, my bottom line is

00:56:40.370 --> 00:56:44.920
whatever it takes for me to
do to make you feel happy.

00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:46.760
So if you want me to say
a dialog, I'll do it,

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:50.104
you want me to take off my
pants and dance, I'll do it.

00:56:50.104 --> 00:56:51.850
(audience cheering)

00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:55.330
See, as long as, as long
as, I'm being very honest.

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:57.320
I can turn around and give you a big spiel

00:56:57.320 --> 00:56:59.930
about what next and the kind of stuff

00:56:59.930 --> 00:57:01.965
that I wanna do, take cinema to the next,

00:57:01.965 --> 00:57:04.580
stretch the boundaries and extend

00:57:04.580 --> 00:57:07.960
the reach of Indian cinema
to global recognition,

00:57:07.960 --> 00:57:09.920
but no, I just wanna make you smile.

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:12.750
So if you tell me, I
have this huge dignity

00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:15.360
of labor thing in me, that whatever I do,

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:18.230
I've done street theater and I've done

00:57:18.230 --> 00:57:19.470
big films like Ra.One.

00:57:19.470 --> 00:57:21.370
It was a dream to bring visual
effects into the country.

00:57:21.370 --> 00:57:23.702
My kids wanted to see a superhero film.

00:57:23.702 --> 00:57:25.720
So I do all that and I never think

00:57:25.720 --> 00:57:27.530
of the business behind it to be honest.

00:57:27.530 --> 00:57:29.600
And whenever I come, wherever I go,

00:57:29.600 --> 00:57:31.740
whichever place I'm in, if people tell me,

00:57:31.740 --> 00:57:33.700
listen Shah Rukh, can you,

00:57:33.700 --> 00:57:36.280
can you say a line from this film?

00:57:36.280 --> 00:57:38.740
And I would, I say yeah
if that makes you happy.

00:57:38.740 --> 00:57:41.220
If somebody tells me can you
just get out of this party,

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:44.172
that'll make me very happy,
I say, I'm gonna go, fine.

00:57:44.172 --> 00:57:47.120
I don't have, yeah I wanna make a studio,

00:57:47.120 --> 00:57:52.120
I wanna make a stadium, I want
to make my cricket team win.

00:57:52.364 --> 00:57:55.870
(audience cheering)

00:57:55.870 --> 00:57:57.766
I've a lot of thoughts, a lot of dreams

00:57:57.766 --> 00:58:00.130
but through all this, I hope I'm able

00:58:00.130 --> 00:58:01.580
to make people smile doing that.

00:58:01.580 --> 00:58:03.640
Even while I lose with the cricket team,

00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:06.120
I hope people smile a
lot while I'm losing.

00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:06.953
So that's the only thing.

00:58:06.953 --> 00:58:08.960
And I think if you keep
it basic and simple,

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:11.513
that's the best way, I think, so yeah.

00:58:12.825 --> 00:58:14.590
I know, I want to make an animation film.

00:58:14.590 --> 00:58:17.460
I want to remain young
to be able to romance

00:58:17.460 --> 00:58:22.460
with my son's girlfriend.
(audience laughing)

00:58:23.751 --> 00:58:24.843
I've got some stupid ideas too.

00:58:25.950 --> 00:58:26.880
- [Nikhil] Thank you.

00:58:26.880 --> 00:58:28.390
- [Jeffrey] Alright,
Sarika, one more quickly.

00:58:28.390 --> 00:58:31.040
- Alright, so your appeal is universal

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:33.670
and it inspires astounding devotion.

00:58:33.670 --> 00:58:35.750
In fact, many of the
audience members here today

00:58:35.750 --> 00:58:38.100
have traveled far distances to see you

00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:40.180
for this short appearance,
and also we'd love

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:42.890
to thank you for traveling
half way around the world

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:47.183
to see just us.
(audience applauding)

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:52.470
You talked a little bit about
this fan base that you have

00:58:52.470 --> 00:58:55.900
among the Germans, and I
heard it's also in Poland,

00:58:55.900 --> 00:58:57.710
so maybe you could talk a little bit

00:58:57.710 --> 00:58:59.795
about what's up with that?

00:58:59.795 --> 00:59:04.200
I also know that you evoke a frenzy

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:08.035
that some people would go so
far as to describe as religious

00:59:08.035 --> 00:59:11.230
and I was wondering how
do you feel about that

00:59:11.230 --> 00:59:15.000
and how does it affect your
mission to create honest art?

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:17.130
And do you think it's sustainable?

00:59:17.130 --> 00:59:19.190
If all of this ends, how do you think

00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:21.610
you'll come to terms with just being

00:59:21.610 --> 00:59:23.963
your honest self, just being human?

00:59:24.895 --> 00:59:26.970
- I'd shoot myself.
(audience laughing)

00:59:26.970 --> 00:59:28.637
No, no.
- I hope not.

00:59:29.710 --> 00:59:31.623
- [Jeffrey] Okay, so
it's not ending, then.

00:59:33.320 --> 00:59:35.500
- I truly believe, I'd be very honest,

00:59:35.500 --> 00:59:36.960
I didn't know how it started.

00:59:36.960 --> 00:59:38.810
I truly believe this is never ending.

00:59:39.807 --> 00:59:42.150
When you say this, that you know,

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:43.910
you have these people in Poland

00:59:43.910 --> 00:59:46.210
and you have in Germany
and Japan, in Malaysia,

00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:48.510
South East Asia, I have woken up,

00:59:48.510 --> 00:59:51.932
I was shooting a film called
One 2 Ka 4 many years ago

00:59:51.932 --> 00:59:56.890
(audience cheering)
in Indonesia and Malaysia.

00:59:56.890 --> 00:59:59.400
I was very tired and I went down to this

00:59:59.400 --> 01:00:01.770
small little house and
I slept on the chair.

01:00:01.770 --> 01:00:04.923
When I woke up, I had this
40 family members sitting.

01:00:06.086 --> 01:00:07.962
They were all looking at me like this,

01:00:07.962 --> 01:00:09.598
so I got up and I was very awkward,

01:00:09.598 --> 01:00:12.430
because I woke up in some
unknown person's house.

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:14.130
When I sleep, when anyone sleeps,

01:00:14.130 --> 01:00:15.617
you don't look to good (snores).

01:00:18.070 --> 01:00:19.566
- [Sarika] Something like that.

01:00:19.566 --> 01:00:21.866
- It looks very, very,
very un-rock star like.

01:00:23.240 --> 01:00:26.402
I was, they had their hands like this,

01:00:26.402 --> 01:00:29.253
they had a little temple with
my picture in their house.

01:00:30.450 --> 01:00:34.030
I was really taken aback
that the stuff that I do,

01:00:34.030 --> 01:00:36.050
which is mostly monkeying around,

01:00:36.050 --> 01:00:38.750
falling down, singing, just having fun

01:00:38.750 --> 01:00:41.260
for eight or 10 hours a day on my sets

01:00:41.260 --> 01:00:43.640
actually invokes this kind of reaction.

01:00:43.640 --> 01:00:46.350
And the first thing I
realized, what you asked me,

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.530
is that the one thing I will never

01:00:48.530 --> 01:00:51.070
ever do is take myself seriously.

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:53.980
I will never try to
intellectualize what I do.

01:00:53.980 --> 01:00:55.790
I would like to be as silly as I was

01:00:55.790 --> 01:00:57.900
when I was four, I would like to do

01:00:57.900 --> 01:01:00.740
exactly the same thing
that I did 20 years ago.

01:01:00.740 --> 01:01:02.510
Because when I go back 20 years ago,

01:01:02.510 --> 01:01:04.220
there's a new set of
audience which is as silly

01:01:04.220 --> 01:01:05.700
and comes and appreciates it.

01:01:05.700 --> 01:01:07.350
Right, so you have to keep on doing that.

01:01:07.350 --> 01:01:10.170
I've never tried to take
myself more seriously.

01:01:10.170 --> 01:01:12.730
Like I was reading it here
when they were showing

01:01:12.730 --> 01:01:14.750
that I've got hundred
awards or being nominated

01:01:14.750 --> 01:01:16.550
for so many, I've got the Padma Shri

01:01:17.444 --> 01:01:20.030
and I have this wonderful fellowship,

01:01:20.030 --> 01:01:22.490
but I'm not going to
ever become more serious

01:01:22.490 --> 01:01:24.120
and intellectual because I have all this.

01:01:24.120 --> 01:01:26.410
I'm going to do exactly the same stuff,

01:01:26.410 --> 01:01:28.390
I'm going to have fun, and that's why

01:01:28.390 --> 01:01:29.980
I believe it'll never end.

01:01:29.980 --> 01:01:32.470
It'll never end because the day I start

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:34.570
intellectualizing and
taking myself seriously,

01:01:34.570 --> 01:01:37.800
my art seriously, and what
you asked me just now,

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:40.300
how are you going to make sure

01:01:40.300 --> 01:01:43.810
that your art is serious and honest,

01:01:43.810 --> 01:01:45.340
I think it's going to be honest

01:01:45.340 --> 01:01:48.160
as long as I'm silly and
stupid and childlike,

01:01:48.160 --> 01:01:51.230
and innocent and naive and an idiot.

01:01:51.230 --> 01:01:53.470
As soon as I become an intellectual

01:01:53.470 --> 01:01:55.470
and I become serious about this stuff,

01:01:55.470 --> 01:01:56.920
I think I'm gonna lose it.

01:01:56.920 --> 01:01:59.123
I'm gonna be sitting somewhere,

01:02:00.790 --> 01:02:02.530
in some big hall giving a lecture

01:02:02.530 --> 01:02:06.193
about how art should be the
most important thing on,

01:02:07.671 --> 01:02:08.943
and I don't wanna do that.

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:12.610
I just wanna keep on
being stupid, that's it.

01:02:12.610 --> 01:02:14.840
I think, to all you kids, please, yeah,

01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:17.840
just be silly and idiotic.
(audience applauding)

01:02:17.840 --> 01:02:21.518
- [Sarika] I can live with that.

01:02:21.518 --> 01:02:25.500
- So, speaking of which.

01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:26.980
You've been sitting
around for a long time.

01:02:26.980 --> 01:02:29.094
You had a long plane ride to get here.

01:02:29.094 --> 01:02:32.094
(audience cheering)

01:02:33.310 --> 01:02:35.904
If you like moving around a little bit?

01:02:35.904 --> 01:02:38.904
(audience cheering)

01:02:40.180 --> 01:02:41.990
So.
- Can I have a mic?

01:02:41.990 --> 01:02:44.050
Because you know what,
because he's not been

01:02:44.050 --> 01:02:46.160
introduced to Hindi cinema properly.

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:46.993
I'm gonna make him say

01:02:46.993 --> 01:02:48.504
a few dialogs.
- You've got a mic.

01:02:48.504 --> 01:02:49.580
- No I want, I want a handle one if I can.

01:02:49.580 --> 01:02:50.530
- Do we have a mic?

01:02:51.641 --> 01:02:53.310
Can we have it, just any mic?

01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:56.650
Because he's got sound,
we just need a mic.

01:02:56.650 --> 01:02:59.391
- Do you wanna fake it?
- Any mic.

01:02:59.391 --> 01:03:02.613
- Take off your shirt!
- No, it's okay.

01:03:02.613 --> 01:03:05.415
Don't tell him to do that, it's not.

01:03:05.415 --> 01:03:07.938
(audience cheering)

01:03:07.938 --> 01:03:12.029
I'm telling you to be
silly, don't be that silly.

01:03:12.029 --> 01:03:13.244
- I've made that mistake.

01:03:13.244 --> 01:03:14.753
(audience laughing)

01:03:14.753 --> 01:03:16.539
Alright, there's no mic.

01:03:16.539 --> 01:03:17.372
- Okay, I'm gonna, because he's.

01:03:17.372 --> 01:03:19.323
- [Staff] Mic, we've got a mic here.

01:03:19.323 --> 01:03:20.240
Microphone.

01:03:22.386 --> 01:03:23.710
- Oh yeah, thank you.

01:03:23.710 --> 01:03:24.947
- Oh, we got a microphone.

01:03:24.947 --> 01:03:25.907
There we go.

01:03:25.907 --> 01:03:29.231
(audience cheering)

01:03:29.231 --> 01:03:30.780
(tapping on mic)

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:35.471
- So, because he's been
introduced to Indian cinema,

01:03:35.471 --> 01:03:37.840
I'm gonna teach him,

01:03:37.840 --> 01:03:40.130
I'm gonna teach him some
of the Indian dialogs

01:03:40.130 --> 01:03:41.980
that I've done from some of my films.

01:03:43.244 --> 01:03:45.550
(audience cheering)

01:03:45.550 --> 01:03:47.710
So you have to say it after me, okay.

01:03:47.710 --> 01:03:49.100
I'm gonna teach you a bit of Hindi.

01:03:49.100 --> 01:03:51.320
So the next time you watch a Hindi film,

01:03:51.320 --> 01:03:52.560
you'll understand it a little better.

01:03:52.560 --> 01:03:55.106
- I do speak German.
- Oh yeah, okay.

01:03:55.106 --> 01:03:56.840
(Jeffrey laughs)
Achtung.

01:03:56.840 --> 01:04:01.737
Alright, so I do a dialog
from Kuch Kuch Hota Hai?

01:04:03.281 --> 01:04:06.402
(audience cheering)

01:04:06.402 --> 01:04:07.235
- Don!
- Or Don.

01:04:07.235 --> 01:04:08.068
Okay, I do Don.

01:04:08.068 --> 01:04:10.795
Don is a film I play the bad guy in,

01:04:10.795 --> 01:04:12.772
so they're saying you
should do it from Don.

01:04:12.772 --> 01:04:14.670
- Am I bad guy?
- You're the bad guy.

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:16.085
- I'm another bad guy.

01:04:16.085 --> 01:04:19.132
- So we need to, here, just stand here.

01:04:19.132 --> 01:04:22.132
(audience cheering)

01:04:23.820 --> 01:04:26.080
That's, I'm gonna use that in my next film

01:04:26.080 --> 01:04:27.430
when I'm playing a bad guy.

01:04:30.300 --> 01:04:32.663
That's cool, yeah.
- We're good, yeah?

01:04:34.508 --> 01:04:37.508
- (speaks in Hindi)

01:04:40.222 --> 01:04:41.610
Too long?

01:04:41.610 --> 01:04:42.910
- Right.
- Okay (laughs).

01:04:45.860 --> 01:04:48.290
Okay, so I'm gonna do the
dialog, I'll do the actions,

01:04:48.290 --> 01:04:50.548
you just do the actions with me, okay?

01:04:50.548 --> 01:04:51.760
And just keep moving your mouth

01:04:51.760 --> 01:04:53.060
so it look like you're speaking.

01:04:53.060 --> 01:04:54.822
- I can do that.

01:04:54.822 --> 01:04:57.180
- (speaks in Hindi)
(audience cheering)

01:04:57.180 --> 01:04:58.013
They love you.

01:05:00.033 --> 01:05:02.866
(speaks in Hindi)

01:05:13.870 --> 01:05:16.227
That's a makeshift gun.
- I see.

01:05:17.710 --> 01:05:21.119
- Do you think, was he cool
being Don or not, tell me.

01:05:21.119 --> 01:05:24.119
(audience cheering)

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:27.990
I'm gonna request him, because,

01:05:27.990 --> 01:05:30.210
okay okay, okay!

01:05:33.690 --> 01:05:35.260
See, that's how Hindi films are,

01:05:35.260 --> 01:05:36.860
they make you silly in a second.

01:05:38.020 --> 01:05:40.220
I'm gonna request everyone,
you guys also come on stage.

01:05:40.220 --> 01:05:42.288
I'll do a little Chammak
Challo for you guys.

01:05:42.288 --> 01:05:45.120
(audience cheering)

01:05:45.120 --> 01:05:46.890
You'll also have to do it with us.

01:05:46.890 --> 01:05:50.143
- We have Yale's best
dancer, Natalia Cosla.

01:05:51.162 --> 01:05:54.079
("Chammak Challo")

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:02.753
Let's do a little background dancing.

01:06:06.534 --> 01:06:09.451
(singing in Hindi)

01:08:20.767 --> 01:08:25.017
(audience cheering and applauding)

01:08:25.937 --> 01:08:27.604
- Good bye everyone.

01:08:39.075 --> 01:08:40.068
- I'm gonna be one second, guys,

01:08:40.068 --> 01:08:42.343
thank you very much.

01:08:42.343 --> 01:08:43.676
Thank you, Nita.

01:08:47.096 --> 01:08:49.140
Before I go, I really
wanna thank all of you

01:08:49.140 --> 01:08:51.360
for, genuinely, from
the bottom of my heart.

01:08:51.360 --> 01:08:54.573
This is the most fun I've had
in the last two, three months.

01:08:57.010 --> 01:08:59.940
So, thank you, Nita.

01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:01.460
Thank you, Isha.

01:09:01.460 --> 01:09:03.337
And all you wonderful
people for coming here

01:09:03.337 --> 01:09:08.290
and being so patient.
(audience cheering)

01:09:08.290 --> 01:09:11.270
I feel with all, because they told me

01:09:11.270 --> 01:09:13.540
this is a serious evening, so I've really,

01:09:13.540 --> 01:09:18.258
really been intellectually
stimulated by this dance.

01:09:18.258 --> 01:09:19.370
(audience laughing)

01:09:19.370 --> 01:09:22.890
I hope you guys have also
been, and I'm so glad

01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:25.203
you take little children into Yale also.

01:09:26.390 --> 01:09:27.540
But thank you so much.

01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:31.070
I can't take you all enough
for your kindness and goodness.

01:09:31.070 --> 01:09:31.903
God bless you.

01:09:33.120 --> 01:09:33.993
And you'll do well, thank you.

